sition and Analysis\",\"Journal of the American College of Cardiology\",\"Journal of the American Dietetic Association\",\"Julieanna Hever\",\"Kaffa Province\",\"Kahawa Sug\",\"Kahlúa\",\"Kahweol\",\"Kaldi\",\"Kapeng barako\",\"Karlsbad coffee maker\",\"Karsk\",\"Kathy Freston\",\"Khat\",\"Kim A. Williams\",\"Knockbox\",\"Kona coffee\",\"Kopi (drink)\",\"Kopi luwak\",\"Kopi tiam\",\"Kopi tubruk\",\"Kris Carr\",\"Kristi Funk\",\"Kurdish coffee\",\"Lacto-ovo vegetarianism\",\"Lacto vegetarianism\",\"Latin\",\"Latin language\",\"Latte\",\"Latte art\",\"Latte macchiato\",\"Laxative\",\"Leaf\",\"Legume\",\"Leonhard Rauwolf\",\"Leucaena\",\"Life Sciences (journal)\",\"Lingzhi (mushroom)\",\"Liqueur coffee\",\"List of bakery cafés\",\"List of chemical compounds in coffee\",\"List of coffee companies\",\"List of coffee dishes\",\"List of coffee drinks\",\"List of coffee varieties\",\"List of coffeehouse chains\",\"List of countries by coffee production\",\"List of culinary fruits\",\"List of culinary herbs and spices\",\"List of culinary nuts\",\"List of edible seeds\",\"List of fermented foods\",\"List of vegan and plant-based media\",\"List of vegetables\",\"Liver\",\"Lomani River\",\"London International Financial Futures and Options Exchange\",\"Long black\",\"Low-carbon diet\",\"Low acid coffee\",\"Low caffeine coffee\",\"Lungo\",\"Maracaturra coffee\",\"Maragogipe Coffee\",\"Margin (economics)\",\"Mark Bittman\",\"Mark Pendergrast\",\"Marocchino\",\"Maronite\",\"Marta Dymek\",\"Martinique\",\"Mary McCartney\",\"Massachusetts Historical Society\",\"Matthew Kenney\",\"Max Gerson\",\"Max La Manna\",\"Maxwell House\",\"Mayo Clinic\",\"Mazagran (drink)\",\"McGraw-Hill\",\"Mediterranean diet\",\"Megan Rossi\",\"Melanoidins\",\"Metabolism\",\"Mia Syn\",\"Michael F. Jacobson\",\"Michael Greger\",\"Michael Klaper\",\"Microfoam\",\"Mike Fremont\",\"Miles Kasiri\",\"Mineral (nutrient)\",\"Moka pot\",\"Mokha\",\"Mold\",\"Molokai coffee\",\"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors\",\"Moretta (coffee)\",\"Mount Marsabit\",\"Mucilage\",\"Mug\",\"Museo del Objeto del Objeto\",\"Muslims\",\"Mycena citricolor\",\"Mysore\",\"NBC News\",\"NPR\",\"Nathan Pritikin\",\"National Arbor Day Foundation\",\"National Coffee Association\",\"National Geographic\",\"Nava Atlas\",\"Neal D. Barnard\",\"Neapolitan flip coffee pot\",\"Nescafé\",\"New Scientist\",\"New Straits Times\",\"New York Mercantile Exchange\",\"Nisha Vora\",\"Nitro cold brew coffee\",\"Nutrient\",\"Nutrients (journal)\",\"Nutritional yeast\",\"OCLC (identifier)\",\"Ocean Robbins\",\"Oliang\",\"Open access\",\"Organic coffee\",\"Orientalism\",\"Oromo people\",\"Osprey Publishing\",\"Ostroveganism\",\"Ottoman Empire\",\"Outcrossing\",\"Ovo vegetarianism\",\"Oxford\",\"Oxford English Dictionary\",\"Oxford University Press\",\"PMC (identifier)\",\"PMID (identifier)\",\"Palaniappan Manickam\",\"Pamela Anderson\",\"Paraxanthine\",\"Peaberry\",\"Per capita\",\"Pera Museum\",\"Pescetarianism\",\"Petiole (botany)\"]},\"ladderLevel\":0,\"innerBudgetRemaining\":99.95,\"probeResult\":{\"pass\":true,\"results\":[]},\"isStable\":false,\"action\":{\"type\":\"NAVIGATE\",\"title\":\"American Revolution\"},\"next\":{\"currentTitle\":\"American Revolution\",\"summary\":\"The American Revolution (1765–1783) was a political conflict involving the Thirteen Colonies and Great Britain, that began as a rebellion demanding reform and evolved into a revolution resulting in a complete separation that entirely replaced the social and political order, as an outcome of the American Revolutionary War and the consequential sovereign independence of the former colonies as the United States. The Second Continental Congress established the Continental Army and appointed George Washington as its commander-in-chief in 1775. The following year, the Congress unanimously adopted the Declaration of Independence. Throughout most of the war, the outcome appeared uncertain. However, in 1781, a decisive victory by Washington and the Continental Army in the Siege of Yorktown led King George III and the British to negotiate the cessation of colonial rule and the acknowledgment of American sovereignty, formalized in the Treaty of Paris in 1783.\\nDiscontent with colonial rule began shortly after the French and Indian War in 1763. Even though the colonies had fought in and supported the war directly with funds and materiel, the British Parliament imposed new taxes to ostensibly compensate for wartime costs and transferred control of the colonies' western lands to British officials in Montreal. Representatives from several colonies convened in New York City for the Stamp Act Congress in 1765; its \\\"Declaration of Rights and Grievances\\\" argued that this taxation without representation and other policies violated their rights as Englishmen. In 1767, though the Stamp Act was repealed, tensions flared again following British Parliament's passage of the Townshend Acts. In an effort to quell the mounting rebellion, King George III deployed British troops to Boston, where they killed antagonists in the 1770 Boston Massacre . In December 1773, the underground Sons of Liberty orchestrated the Boston Tea Party, during which they dumped chests of taxed tea owned by the British East India Company into Boston Harbor. Parliament responded by enacting a series of punitive laws, which effectively ended self-government in Massachusetts but also intensified support for the revolutionary cause among Americans.\\nIn 1774, twelve of the Thirteen Colonies sent delegates to the First Continental Congress; the Province of Georgia joined in 1775. The First Continental Congress began coordinating Patriot resistance through underground networks of committees largely built on the foundations of the Sons of Liberty. In August 1775, King George III proclaimed Massachusetts to be in a state of rebellion. The British attempted to disarm the colonists, resulting in the Battles of Lexington and Concord, sparking the Revolutionary War. The Continental Army then surrounded Boston, forcing the British to withdraw by sea in March 1776, and leaving Patriots in control in every colony.  In May 1776, Congress voted to suppress all forms of Crown authority, to be replaced by locally created authority, and each colony created a state constitution. On July 2, the Congress passed the Lee Resolution, affirming their support for joint independence, and on July 4, 1776 they unanimously adopted the Declaration of Independence, having evolved into a revolution now basing their claims on universal rights and famously proclaiming that \\\"all men are created equal\\\". The Second Continental Congress soon after began deliberating the Articles of Confederation, an effort to establish a multi-state self-governing coordinating body capable of negotiating international treaties and prosecuting the war.\\nThe Revolutionary War continued for another five years during which France ultimately entered, supporting the revolutionary cause. On September 28, 1781, Washington led the Continental Army's most decisive victory at the Siege of Yorktown, leading to the collapse of King George's control of Parliament. Consensus in Parliament soon shifted to the war ending on American terms. On September 3, 1783, the British signed the Treaty of Paris, ceding to the new nation nearly all the territory east of the Mississippi River and south of the Great Lakes. The United States became the first nation to establish a federal republic with a written constitution based on the principles of universal natural rights, consent of the governed and equality under the law, albeit with significant democratic limitations compared to later evolution of the concept.\\n\\n\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American%20Revolution\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American Revolution\"],\"depth\":1,\"links\":[\"11 September 1922 Revolution\",\"14 July Revolution\",\"1689 Boston revolt\",\"1776 (book)\",\"1788–89 United States presidential election\",\"1791 State of the Union Address\",\"1792 State of the Union Address\",\"1792 United States presidential election\",\"1793 State of the Union Address\",\"1794 State of the Union Address\",\"1795 State of the Union Address\",\"1796 State of the Union Address\",\"1860 United States presidential election\",\"1911 Revolution\",\"1935 Greek coup d'état attempt\",\"1946 Italian institutional referendum\",\"1952 Egyptian revolution\",\"1960 South African republic referendum\",\"1969 Libyan coup d'état\",\"1970 Cambodian coup d'état\",\"1980 United States presidential election\",\"1984 United States presidential election\",\"1987 Fijian coups d'état\",\"1988 United States presidential election\",\"1992 Los Angeles riots\",\"1993 World Trade Center bombing\",\"1st Rhode Island Regiment\",\"2005 Atlantic Hurricane Season\",\"2010 Kyrgyz Revolution\",\"2011 Egyptian revolution\",\"2011 Tucson shooting\",\"2012 Aurora theater shooting\",\"2012 Benghazi attack\",\"2016 United States presidential election\",\"2017 Atlantic Hurricane Season\",\"2017 Las Vegas shooting\",\"2019 El Paso shooting\",\"2020–2021 U.S. troop withdrawal from Afghanistan\",\"2023 United States banking crisis\",\"2025 shootings of Minnesota legislators\",\"27 colonial grievances\",\"50 State Quarters\",\"5 October 1910 revolution\",\"A Full Vindication of the Measures of Congress\",\"A More Perfect Union (film)\",\"A Summary View of the Rights of British America\",\"Abigail Adams\",\"Abolition of monarchy\",\"Abolitionism in the United States\",\"Abortion-rights movement\",\"Abortion debate\",\"Abortion in the United States\",\"Abraham Baldwin\",\"Abraham Clark\",\"Abraham Lincoln\",\"Adam Smith\",\"Adamantios Korais\",\"Administration of Justice Act 1774\",\"Admiralty court\",\"Admission to the Union\",\"Affirmative action in the United States\",\"Affluence in the United States\",\"Africa Liberal Network\",\"African-American history\",\"African American history\",\"African Americans in the 