nt\",\"Civil rights movement (1865–1896)\",\"Civil rights movement (1896–1954)\",\"Civil society\",\"Civil township\",\"Civil war\",\"Class struggle\",\"Classical Athens\",\"Classical liberalism\",\"Classical radicalism\",\"Classical republicanism\",\"Climate change in the United States\",\"Clive Lewis (politician)\",\"Coat of arms of the Washington family\",\"Code of Federal Regulations\",\"Coinage Act of 1792\",\"Cold War\",\"Cold War (1947–1948)\",\"Cold War (1948–1953)\",\"Cold War (1953–1962)\",\"Cold War (1962–1979)\",\"Cold War (1979–1985)\",\"Cold War (1985–1991)\",\"Colonial American military history\",\"Colonial India\",\"Colonial Williamsburg\",\"Colonial charters in the Thirteen Colonies\",\"Colonial government in the Thirteen Colonies\",\"Colonial history of the United States\",\"Colony\",\"Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations\",\"Colony of Virginia\",\"Colorado River\",\"Colour revolution\",\"Columbia (name)\",\"Columbia (personification)\",\"Columbia River\",\"Columbine High School massacre\",\"Comanche history\",\"Commanding General of the United States Army\",\"Commemoration of the American Revolution\",\"Commerce Clause\",\"Committee of Detail\",\"Committee of Five\",\"Committee of Secret Correspondence\",\"Committee of safety (American Revolution)\",\"Committees of Correspondence\",\"Committees of correspondence\",\"Common Sense\",\"Common Sense (pamphlet)\",\"Common good\",\"Commonwealth of England\",\"Communications in the United States\",\"Communist revolution\",\"Communitarianism\",\"Comparison of U.S. state and territory governments\",\"Compromise of 1850\",\"Compromise of 1877\",\"Compulsory Process Clause\",\"Conceived in Liberty\",\"Conciliatory Resolution\",\"Concurrent powers\",\"Confederate States of America\",\"Confederation\",\"Confederation of United States of America\",\"Confederation period\",\"Confrontation Clause\",\"Congregationalism in the United States\",\"Congress Voting Independence\",\"Congress of the Confederation\",\"Congressional Apportionment Amendment\",\"Congressional Budget Office\",\"Congressional Gold Medal\",\"Congressional power of enforcement\",\"Connecticut Colony\",\"Connecticut Compromise\",\"Consent of the governed\",\"Conservative liberalism\",\"Consolidated city-county\",\"Constitution Day and Citizenship Day\",\"Constitution Gardens\",\"Constitution Week\",\"Constitution of Delaware\",\"Constitution of Maryland\",\"Constitution of Massachusetts\",\"Constitution of New Hampshire\",\"Constitution of New Jersey\",\"Constitution of New York\",\"Constitution of Pennsylvania\",\"Constitution of Virginia\",\"Constitution of the United Kingdom\",\"Constitution of the United States\",\"Constitutional Convention (United States)\",\"Constitutional law of the United States\",\"Constitutional liberalism\",\"Constitutional monarchy\",\"Contentious politics\",\"Contiguous United States\",\"Continental Army\",\"Continental Association\",\"Continental Congress\",\"Continental Currency dollar coin\",\"Continental Marines\",\"Continental Navy\",\"Continental Union Flag\",\"Continental currency banknotes\",\"Contingent election\",\"Contract Clause\",\"Convention to propose amendments to the United States Constitution\",\"Conway Cabal\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"American Revolution\"},\"ladderLevel\":0.4067604802966357,\"innerBudgetRemaining\":99.80000000000001,\"probeResult\":{\"pass\":true,\"results\":[]},\"isStable\":false,\"action\":{\"type\":\"NAVIGATE\",\"title\":\"14 July Revolution\"},\"next\":{\"currentTitle\":\"14 July Revolution\",\"summary\":\"The 14 July Revolution, also known as the 1958 Iraqi military coup, was a bloody military coup that took place on 14 July 1958 in Iraq, resulting in the toppling of King Faisal II and the overthrow of the Hashemite-led Kingdom of Iraq. The  Iraqi Republic established in its wake ended the Hashemite Arab Federation between Iraq and Jordan that had been established just six months earlier.\\nThe Kingdom of Iraq had been a hotbed of Arab nationalism since the Second World War. Unrest mounted amid economic malaise and widespread disapproval of Western influence, which was exacerbated by the formation of the Baghdad Pact in 1955, as well as Faisal's support of the British-led invasion of Egypt during the Suez Crisis. Prime Minister Nuri al-Said's policies were unpopular, particularly within the military ranks. Opposition groups began to organize in secret, modelling themselves after the Egyptian Free Officers Movement that overthrew the Egyptian monarchy in 1952. Pan-Arabic sentiment in Iraq was further bolstered by the creation of the United Arab Republic in February 1958 under the leadership of Gamal Abdel Nasser, a staunch proponent of anti-imperialist causes.\\nIn July 1958, units of the Royal Iraqi Army were dispatched to Jordan in support of King Hussein. A group of Iraqi Free Officers, led by Brigadier Abd al-Karim Qasim and Colonel Abdul Salam Arif, took advantage of the opportunity and instead marched on Baghdad. On 14 July, revolutionary forces seized control of the capital and proclaimed a new republic, headed by a Revolutionary Council. King Faisal and Crown Prince Abd al-Ilah were executed at the royal Al-Rehab Palace, bringing an end to the Hashemite dynasty in Iraq. Prime Minister al-Said attempted to flee but was captured and shot a day later. After the coup, Qasim assumed the position of Prime Minister and Minister of Defence, while Arif was named Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Interior. A provisional constitution was adopted in late July. By March 1959, the new Iraqi government had withdrawn from the Baghdad Pact and aligned itself with the Soviet Union.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14%20July%20Revolution\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American Revolution\",\"14 July Revolution\"],\"depth\":2,\"links\":[\"'Abd al-Ilah\",\"11 September 1922 Revolution\",\"12-3 incident\",\"15 August 1975 Bangladeshi coup d'état\",\"17