istory of the Catholic Church\",\"Economic history of France\",\"Economy of France\",\"Edict of Nantes\",\"Edmund Burke\",\"Education in France\",\"Edward Pellew, 1st Viscount Exmouth\",\"Egalitarianism\",\"Elections in France\",\"Eleftherios Venizelos\",\"Elin Jones\",\"Elysée Loustallot\",\"Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès\",\"Emmanuel de Grouchy, marquis de Grouchy\",\"Energy in France\",\"English Revolution\",\"Enlightenment Spain\",\"Enragés\",\"Estates General of 1789\",\"Estates General of 1789 in France\",\"Estates of the realm\",\"Ethel Mannin\",\"Eureka Rebellion\",\"Euro\",\"Euro area crisis\",\"Euromaidan\",\"Euronext Paris\",\"European integration\",\"European wars of religion\",\"Eustache Charles d'Aoust\",\"Execution of Louis XVI\",\"Exsurge Domine\",\"Extrajudicial killings\",\"Fall of Maximilien Robespierre\",\"Fall of the Western Roman Empire\",\"Farm (revenue leasing)\",\"Federal republic\",\"Ferme générale\",\"Festa della Repubblica\",\"Feudal guild system\",\"Feudalism\",\"Feuillant (political group)\",\"First Battle of Wissembourg\",\"First Battle of Zurich\",\"First Bulgarian Empire\",\"First Council of Constantinople\",\"First Council of Lyon\",\"First Council of Nicaea\",\"First Council of the Lateran\",\"First French Empire\",\"First Party System\",\"First Vatican Council\",\"First White Terror\",\"First seven ecumenical councils\",\"Flag of France\",\"Flanders\",\"Flight to Varennes\",\"Foreign relations of France\",\"Fourth Council of Constantinople (Catholic Church)\",\"Fourth Council of the Lateran\",\"France\",\"France during World War II\",\"France in the Middle Ages\",\"France in the long nineteenth century\",\"Francia\",\"Francis Xavier\",\"Francis of Assisi\",\"Franks\",\"François-Marie, marquis de Barthélemy\",\"François-Nicolas Vincent\",\"François-Noël Babeuf\",\"François-René de Chateaubriand\",\"François Antoine de Boissy d'Anglas\",\"François Chabot\",\"François Christophe de Kellermann\",\"François Claude Amour, marquis de Bouillé\",\"François Furet\",\"François Guizot\",\"François Hanriot\",\"François Joseph Lefebvre\",\"François Mignet\",\"François Sébastien Charles Joseph de Croix, Count of Clerfayt\",\"François Séverin Marceau\",\"François Victor Alphonse Aulard\",\"François de La Rochefoucauld, 7th Duke of La Rochefoucauld\",\"Frederick Louis, Prince of Hohenlohe-Ingelfingen\",\"Frederick William II of Prussia\",\"Free France\",\"Free people of color\",\"Freehold (law)\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"French Revolution\"},\"feedback\":3.070203592027472},{\"t\":3,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"French Revolution\",\"summary\":\"The French Revolution was a period of political and societal change in France that began with the Estates General of 1789 and ended with the Coup of 18 Brumaire on 9 November 1799. Many of the revolution's ideas are considered fundamental principles of liberal democracy, and its values remain central to modern French political discourse. It was caused by a combination of social, political, and economic factors which the existing regime proved unable to manage.  \\nFinancial crisis and widespread social distress led to the convocation of the Estates General in May 1789, its first meeting since 1614. The representatives of the Third Estate broke away and re-constituted themselves as a National Assembly in June. The Storming of the Bastille in Paris on 14 July led to a series of radical measures by the Assembly, including the abolition of feudalism, state control over the Catholic Church in France, and issuing the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen. \\nThe next three years were dominated by a struggle for political control. King Louis XVI's attempted flight to Varennes in June 1791 further discredited the monarchy, and military defeats after the outbreak of the French Revolutionary Wars in April 1792 led to the insurrection of 10 August 1792. As a result, the monarchy was replaced by the French First Republic in September, followed by the execution of Louis XVI himself in January 1793.\\nAfter another revolt in June 1793, the constitution was suspended, and political power passed from the National Convention to the Committee of Public Safety, dominated by radical Jacobins led by Maximilien Robespierre. About 16,000 people were sentenced by the Revolutionary Tribunal and executed in the Reign of Terror, which ended in July 1794 with the Thermidorian Reaction. Weakened by external threats and internal opposition, the Committee of Public Safety was replaced in November 1795 by the Directory. Its instability ended in 1799 with the coup of 18 Brumaire and the establishment of the Consulate, with Napoleon Bonaparte as First Consul.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French%20Revolution\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American Revolution\",\"14 July Revolution\",\"French Revolution\"],\"depth\":3,\"links\":[\"11 September 1922 Revolution\",\"13 Vendémiaire\",\"14 July Revolution\",\"1791 French legislative election\",\"1792 French National Convention election\",\"1795 French legislative election\",\"1798 French legislative election\",\"1851 French coup d'état\",\"18 Brumaire\",\"1911 Revolution\",\"1935 Greek coup d'état attempt\",\"1946 Italian institutional referendum\",\"1952 Egyptian revolution\",\"1960 South African republic referendum\",\"1969 Libyan coup d'état\",\"1970 Cambodian coup d'état\",\"1987 Fijian coups d'état\",\"2005 French riots\",\"2010 Kyrgyz Revolution\",\"2011 Egyptian revolution\",\"2017 French presidential election\",\"2021 French labor protests\",\"2022 French presidential election\",\"2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine\",\"5 October 1910 revolution\",\"A Discourse on the Love of Our Country\",\"A Vindication of the Rights of Men\",\"Abolition of feudalism in France\",\"Abolition of monarchy\",\"Abortion in France\",\"Abraham Lincoln\",\"Absolute monarchy\",\"Absolute monarchy in France\",\"Action of 13 January 1797\",\"Action of 25 January 1797\",\"Active citizenship